<div class="article-body">

<p>I spent 2 weeks testing everything before realizing the problem was my pH meter. It was off by 1.2 points. My lettuce looked like a dying plant when the real issue was a $12 tool I never thought to question.</p>

<p>Yellow leaves in hydroponics is the #1 question beginners ask. And there's a good reason. it can be caused by 6 different things, and they all look similar at first. I've personally dealt with every single one on this list. Here's the diagnosis flow I use now, in order of what to check first.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I'm not a scientist. I'm just someone who killed a lot of plants before figuring out the pattern. If I can save you the 3 months it took me to learn this, this article did its job.</p>

<h2 id="nutrient">1. Nutrient Deficiency: The Most Common Cause</h2>

<p>When I first saw yellow leaves, I thought it was a disease. Turns out the plant was just hungry. In hydroponics, the plant relies 100% on what you put in the water. Miss a nutrient, and the leaves show it within days.</p>

<p>Here's how to read the symptoms:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Nitrogen deficiency</strong>. Older leaves turn pale yellow starting from the tips. New growth stays green. This was my first failure. I was using half the recommended dose because I thought "less is safer."</li>
  <li><strong>Iron deficiency</strong>. New leaves turn yellow but the veins stay green (called chlorosis). I see this when the pH drifts above 6.5, locking out iron.</li>
  <li><strong>Magnesium deficiency</strong>. Yellowing between the veins on older leaves. Looks like a tiger stripe pattern. I fixed this with calmag in 3 days.</li>
</ul>

<p>Fix it fast: use a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=complete+hydroponic+nutrient+kit+beginner&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">complete hydroponic nutrient kit</a> designed for leafy greens, not tomatoes. Follow the label. I use General Hydroponics Flora Series at half strength for lettuce, full strength for basil. If you're seeing the tiger-stripe pattern, a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=calmag+supplement+hydroponics&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Cal-Mag supplement</a> usually fixes it within a week.</p>

<p>Check out the <a href="/posts/hydroponic-nutrient-guide">Hydroponic Nutrient Guide</a> for the exact mix I use.</p>

<h2 id="ph">2. pH Imbalance: The Silent Killer</h2>

<p>This is the one that got me. My nutrients were perfect on paper. The EC was right. The water temp was right. But my pH meter was uncalibrated and reading 6.8 when the actual pH was 4.6. My plants were drowning in acid while I stared at yellow leaves wondering what I did wrong.</p>

<p>pH determines whether roots can absorb nutrients. If the pH is wrong, it doesn't matter how perfect your nutrient mix is. the plant can't eat. It's like having a fridge full of food but the door is locked.</p>

<p>For hydroponics, keep pH between <strong>5.5 and 6.5</strong>. Most leafy greens do best at 5.8-6.2.</p>

<p>Fix it: Get a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=pH+meter+for+hydroponics+calibration&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">reliable pH meter with calibration solution</a>. I use the Apera PH60 after burning through 3 cheap $15 meters that drifted within a month. Calibrate every 2 weeks. It sounds annoying, but it takes 2 minutes and saves you from buying replacement plants.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Math</div>
  <p>A $40 pH meter lasts 2+ years if maintained. A single failed batch of plants costs $20-50 in seeds and nutrients. The meter pays for itself in one grow cycle.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="oxygen">3. Low Oxygen at the Roots</h2>

<p>Roots need oxygen. Simple fact, but easy to overlook when you're new. I almost gave up after my first DWC setup because I thought the bubbles from the airstone looked "strong enough." They weren't. My roots were gray, not white, and the leaves turned yellow from the bottom up.</p>

<p>This is especially common with the Kratky method. if you refill too high, the air gap disappears and the roots suffocate. I killed 3 basil plants this way before I learned: the water level should be about 1 inch below the net pot, not touching the roots.</p>

<p>For DWC systems: keep water temps at <strong>65-70°F (18-21°C)</strong>. Warmer water holds less oxygen. If your air pump is weak, upgrade to a <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=hydroponic+air+pump+4+outlet&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">stronger air pump with dual outlets</a>. I switched to an 8W pump and saw greener leaves in 4 days.</p>

<h2 id="root-rot">4. Root Rot: The One That Spreads</h2>

<p>Yellow leaves plus brown, slimy, smelly roots? That's root rot. It's the worst-case scenario. I lost an entire batch of kale to root rot because I was too slow to act.</p>

<p>Root rot thrives in warm, stagnant water with low oxygen. Once it takes hold, it spreads through shared nutrient solutions fast. I've seen a single infected plant take out a whole shelf in 5 days.</p>

<p>How to fix it:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Remove severely affected plants immediately</li>
  <li>Drain and sanitize the whole system with bleach or hydrogen peroxide</li>
  <li>Add beneficial bacteria like Hydroguard to the new nutrient solution</li>
  <li>Drop water temp to 65°F and max out aeration</li>
</ul>

<p>The old yellow leaves probably won't recover, but new growth should look healthy within a week. Focus on the new leaves, not the damaged ones.</p>

<h2 id="light">5. Too Much or Too Little Light</h2>

<p>I made both mistakes. First light was too weak. my lettuce stretched and turned pale yellow looking for photons. I upgraded to a stronger light and placed it 4 inches above the plants. Within 3 days, the top leaves were bleached white.</p>

<p>Seedlings need gentle light. Mature plants need more. If you see pale, stretched growth, the light is too far or too weak. If you see burnt tips or bleached tops, it's too close or too strong.</p>

<p>For most hydroponic greens: 14-16 hours of light per day at 12-18 inches for mature plants, 18-24 inches for seedlings. If you're shopping for lights, check the <a href="https://growlightdb.tabletopharvest.com" target="_blank">Grow Light DB</a> to compare specs and prices.</p>

<h2 id="aging">6. Natural Leaf Aging (It Can Be Normal)</h2>

<p>Here's the one nobody tells you: if only the oldest, lowest leaves are yellow and everything else looks healthy, it might just be the plant getting old. I panicked over this for a week before realizing my lettuce was just maturing.</p>

<p>Plants shed old leaves. It's normal. Remove them to improve airflow and let the plant focus energy on new growth. The key difference: <strong>natural aging affects the bottom leaves first, and only the bottom leaves.</strong> If the yellowing is spreading upward, it's one of the other 5 causes.</p>

<h2 id="faq">Quick Reference: FAQ and Diagnosis Cheat Sheet</h2>

<p>Here are the questions I see most often from beginners, answered directly:</p>

<p><strong>Can tap water cause yellow leaves?</strong> Yes. Hard water, chlorine, or chloramine can block nutrient uptake. If your plants are yellowing and you're using tap water, try switching to filtered or dechlorinated water for a week and see if new growth looks greener.</p>

<p><strong>How fast will plants recover after I fix the issue?</strong> New growth should look healthy within 5-7 days. The old yellow leaves usually won't turn green again. Don't wait for them. if the new leaves look good, you fixed it.</p>

<p><strong>Should I flush my system if I notice sudden yellowing?</strong> If you suspect nutrient burn or salt buildup, yes. Run plain pH-balanced water for 24 hours, then refill with fresh nutrients at half strength. I've saved plants this way after overfeeding.</p>

<p><strong>What deficiency causes yellow leaves first?</strong> Nitrogen is the most common. If the bottom leaves are yellow and the top is green, start with nitrogen. This is the default answer for 60% of yellow leaf problems.</p>

<p><strong>How do I know if it's pH vs deficiency?</strong> Check pH first. It takes 30 seconds with a meter. If pH is outside 5.5-6.5, fix that before touching nutrients. I wasted 2 weeks adding nutrients to a problem that was purely pH.</p>

